From ff72ca3d7c0f9089afa6df9b41ec89c78804a4b1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Giuseppe Maggio <jertlok@proton.me>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 10:17:56 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] mobile-providers: resolve the MVNOs sharing an MCC/MNC

An MCC/MNC pair identifies a network, not a provider. Every MVNO reselling
that network reports the same one, and the database lists them as siblings
of the host network, so the lookup had several equally good candidates and
returned whichever the hash iteration reached first. In practice that is the
most recently added one, which is systematically the MVNO -- host networks
have been in the database since the beginning.

So an Italian subscriber is offered internet.postemobile.it, and a
T-Mobile US subscriber pwg, which is US Mobile's. Neither works.

The database already carries what is needed to do better, and has for years:

  - provider primary="true|false" says which provider owns the MCC/MNC and
    which ones piggy-back on it. It was added in 2023 specifically so that
    clients could stop guessing (mobile-broadband-provider-info!105), and
    43 providers are tagged today. Nothing has ever read it.
  - gsm/gid1 records the EF_GID1 of an MVNO's SIMs (!141).

Score the candidates the way Android's CarrierResolver does: a match on a
higher tier beats any match that does not reach that tier, and anything the
database does not state about a provider is a wildcard. With no MVNO data at
all every candidate scores alike and the result is the MCC/MNC match it has
always been, so older databases keep working unchanged.

Add nma_mobile_providers_database_lookup_3gpp_sim() for callers that can read
the SIM. libnma has no business talking to ModemManager, so the SIM's fields
are passed in, as CarrierResolver passes its subscription rule into match().
EF_GID1 is matched as a case-insensitive prefix because SIMs pad it with
filler the database does not record -- again what Android does. The provider
name doubles as EF_SPN, which is what MVNOs are named after; unlike gid1 it
is a display name rather than a declared matcher, so a mismatch only fails to
earn the tier instead of ruling the provider out.

Against the database shipped today this changes 17 MCC/MNC pairs in 8
countries, every one of them from a reseller to the network it resells:
Ting and US Mobile to T-Mobile, simyo to E-Plus, nju mobile to Orange,
Mineo and OCN to NTTdocomo, Bip to 3, and so on. The remaining 779 are
unchanged.
